WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.959
Floria Sigismondi's The Turning doesn't really
have an ending.

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:08.830
It just stops, leaving viewers to puzzle over
the surreal images that close out the film.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:13.179
If you, like everyone else, were baffled by
the horror flick's bizarre ending, we're here

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.179
to help.

00:00:14.179 --> 00:00:15.179
A tale of two endings

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:18.340
The Turning offers two explanations for the
spooky events at Bly Manor.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:23.380
Either Bly is haunted by the ghosts of its
late stablemaster, Quint, and Flora's former

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.119
governess, Miss Jessel, or Kate has gone completely
crazy.

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:28.660
In a sense, the film has it both ways.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:33.021
After Kate receives a package full of paintings
from her mother, who's mentally ill, The Turning

00:00:33.021 --> 00:00:36.330
quickly devolves into a frantic chase through
a haunted house.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.460
In this version, everything has a clear and
obvious answer.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.550
Quint assaulted Miss Jessel, then killed her
when she tried to flee.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.570
Bly Manor's caretaker, Mrs. Grose, killed
Quint a few weeks later.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.870
Quint and Miss Jessel's spirits now haunt
the manor, and when the ghost of Quint turns

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.900
violent, Kate loads the kids into the car
and flees.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:53.900
Simple, right?

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.860
"It's not that simple."

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.430
Here's the twist: Once Kate escapes Bly, the
film cuts back to the scene in which she's

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.080
looking at her mother's paintings.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.200
It ends differently.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.010
This time, instead of running, Kate confronts
the children, clearly insane.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.370
Miles taunts Kate, and then Kate has a vision
of her mother painting.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.620
As Mom lifts her head to Kate, Kate screams.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:13.890
Cue credits.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.410
The way the film is structured implies that
the second ending is the "real" one.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.860
At the end of the movie, Kate is insane.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.590
It's not clear if the first ending was a prophetic
vision or crazy person's fantasy, and we don't

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.280
know if Bly is actually haunted.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.360
It doesn't matter.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.200
Either way, Kate has lost her mind.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.300
Going back to the source

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.260
So, what are we supposed to do with that information?

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.400
The original text might hold some clues.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.730
The Turning is actually an adaptation of Henry
James' novella The Turn of the Screw, which

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:43.930
was published in 1898, and has been a popular
ghost story ever since, with several film

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.490
and TV adaptations.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:49.870
On the surface, The Turning and The Turn of
the Screw have the same plot: A young woman

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:53.680
moves to an old estate, where she's charged
with teaching an orphaned girl.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.070
The girl's big brother arrives home unexpectedly
after being expelled from his boarding school.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:02.250
The governess begins to see the ghosts of
Quint and Miss Jessel, and becomes convinced

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.730
that the kids do, too.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:06.370
However, in the book, the ending is very different.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:10.010
In the novella, the kids are very clearly
haunted by two ghosts.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.340
Flora is freed from Miss Jessel's spirit and
escapes.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.110
However, when the governess tries to make
Miles explain why he was expelled, Quint appears.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:20.300
The governess breaks Quint's hold on Miles,
but the boy dies.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.921
If you take the text at face value, then the
ghosts at Bly Manor are very real but, as

00:02:24.921 --> 00:02:26.270
you'll see, that's a big "if."

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:27.530
Following in Henry James' footsteps

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.849
Of course, while The Turn of the Screw's ending
seems a lot clearer than The Turning's, ambiguity

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:34.150
is one of this story's oldest traditions.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:39.240
As early as 1907, one critic suggested that
The Turn of the Screw's ghosts aren't real.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.500
Instead, the argument goes, everything supernatural
happens in the governess' mind.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.770
She killed Miles, not some evil spirit.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:47.620
The blood is on her hands.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:48.709
Some people agree.

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:52.430
Others don't, defending the governess and
insisting that we're supposed to take The

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.430
Turn of the Screw at face value.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:58.650
None of Henry James' other ghost stories play
fast and loose with the narrator's sanity,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:02.510
and nothing he said about the novella later
suggests that we shouldn't take the governess

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:03.510
at her word.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.270
It's a debate that continues to this day,
although the discussion kind of misses the

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:08.270
point.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:09.270
James scared his audience.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.849
That was his real goal.

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:15.630
The Turning's weird and confusing ending seems
to be director Floria Sigismondi's attempt

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.370
to capture that same ambiguity.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.540
No, that doesn't explain exactly what happened
at the conclusion of the film, but at least

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.770
it explains why the filmmaker decided to end
things on such an odd note.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:27.380
There's a clue in the title, perhaps.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.280
In the original novella, the phrase "turn
of the screw" appears twice.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.129
Turning, or tightening, a screw increases
tension.

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:38.760
The first time James uses it, he's talking
about how to make a scary story even more

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.760
frightening.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.690
The second time, the governess is discussing
how the events at Bly Manor are testing her

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:44.690
sanity.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:48.129
By calling the film The Turning, perhaps the
filmmakers are emphasizing that the story

00:03:48.129 --> 00:03:53.129
is really about Kate's descent into madness,
and the ghost question is secondary, and eerily

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:55.000
ambiguous, just like in the novella.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.090
Lingering questions

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.739
It's also very hard to rule out the existence
of the ghosts entirely.

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:03.530
Kate starts hearing voices almost as soon
as she moves into the manor, before Miles

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.019
starts playing psychological games with her.

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:07.920
Flora draws pictures of Miss Jessel.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.200
The mannequin modeled after Miles and Flora's
ancestor moves its head after Kate leaves

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:13.200
the room.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.879
We're not seeing that through Kate's eyes,
so it seems like it's real.

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:16.879
If it's not?

00:04:16.879 --> 00:04:18.719
That's pretty sloppy storytelling.

00:04:18.719 --> 00:04:21.339
Maybe The Turning's ghosts are real and maybe
they're not.

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:22.849
In the end, they don't matter.

00:04:22.849 --> 00:04:24.990
Like her mother before her, Kate goes crazy.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.300
That's the real horror in the story.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.180
Of course, that doesn't explain everything
that happens in the movie.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.360
Was the first ending all in Kate's head, or
was there more to it?

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.020
Does Kate's mother have prophetic abilities,
as The Turning subtly hints?

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.759
What exactly happened between Miss Jessel
and Quint, and how did Quint's mysterious

00:04:40.759 --> 00:04:42.020
accident occur?

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.969
The Turning offers answers to some of these
questions, but actively refuses to commit

00:04:45.969 --> 00:04:46.969
to any of them.

00:04:46.969 --> 00:04:48.460
It's confusing and a little off-putting.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:53.789
Ultimately, The Turning probably works best
as a story about woman's descent into madness,

00:04:53.789 --> 00:04:57.860
aided by a sadistic child and a bad draw at
the genetic lottery.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.289
As a ghost story, it's pretty run-of-the-mill.

00:05:00.289 --> 00:05:06.069
As a cinematic exercise in frustrating mainstream
horror audiences, it succeeds, wildly.

